{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"f31ed43a-7dda-4e7d-bd77-4aa806de9b41","title":"The complement of an event in a Venn diagram"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f31ed43a-7dda-4e7d-bd77-4aa806de9b41","step":{"id":"s1","script":"The rectangle represents every possible outcome, called the sample space. The circle contains the outcomes in event A.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"The complement of an event"},{"op":"figure","id":"venn","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-4.5,4.5],"y":[-3,3],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"polyline","id":"sample","points":[[-4,-2.5],[4,-2.5],[4,2.5],[-4,2.5]],"closed":true},{"kind":"circle","id":"event","center":[-0.6,0],"r":1.6},{"kind":"text","at":[-0.6,0],"text":"A"},{"kind":"text","at":[3.5,2.1],"text":"S"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f31ed43a-7dda-4e7d-bd77-4aa806de9b41","step":{"id":"s2","script":"The complement of A contains every outcome in the sample space that is not in A. Shade outside the circle, but inside the rectangle.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"shading","on":"venn","marks":[{"kind":"shade","inside":"sample","except":["event"]},{"kind":"text","at":[2.5,0],"text":"not A"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"notation","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A^c = \\{\\text{outcomes in }S\\text{ not in }A\\}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f31ed43a-7dda-4e7d-bd77-4aa806de9b41","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Every outcome belongs either to A or to its complement, never both. Their probabilities therefore add to one.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"outcomes","on":"venn","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"inside","at":[-1,0.6],"label":"in A"},{"kind":"point","id":"outside","at":[2,-1],"label":"in Aᶜ"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sum","anchor":{"el":"notation","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"P(A)+P(A^c)=1"},{"op":"equation","id":"rule","anchor":{"el":"sum","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"P(A^c)=1-P(A)"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f31ed43a-7dda-4e7d-bd77-4aa806de9b41","step":{"id":"s4","script":"If A has probability zero point three, its complement has probability zero point seven. Complement means all the remaining outcomes, not just one alternative.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"example","anchor":{"el":"rule","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"P(A)=0.3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad P(A^c)=0.7"},{"op":"figure","id":"values","on":"venn","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[-0.6,-0.6],"text":"0.3"},{"kind":"text","at":[2.5,0.7],"text":"0.7"}]}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If P(A) = 0.8, what is P(Aᶜ)?","expected_gist":"0.2, because the complement has probability 1 minus 0.8."}}}
